WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.320
All right.

00:04.320 --> 00:07.120
So now we're going to talk about the second group of operators.

00:07.120 --> 00:09.520
And we're gonna start with the in operator.

00:09.520 --> 00:10.840
So what is an operator.

00:11.000 --> 00:16.720
As we learned before in the comparison operators, we can go and filter the data based on only one single

00:16.720 --> 00:17.200
value.

00:17.200 --> 00:22.560
But now in some scenarios, we have to go and filter the data based on multiple values, not only one.

00:22.560 --> 00:25.400
In this case we can go and use the n operator.

00:25.400 --> 00:30.760
So if we go and use the n operator, it's going to go and check whether the value matches any value

00:30.760 --> 00:31.680
from a list.

00:31.680 --> 00:35.360
So a list of multiple values if it matches any of them.

00:35.360 --> 00:37.120
So we will get true okay.

00:37.160 --> 00:42.080
So now let's have a quick look to the syntax of the subquery using the in operator.

00:42.080 --> 00:46.520
So we start with the classic stuff where we say okay we would like to retrieve the column one column

00:46.520 --> 00:48.360
two from the table one.

00:48.640 --> 00:52.640
And we want to filter the data based on the column from the table one.

00:52.640 --> 00:56.720
Now after specifying the column we're going to use the in operator.

00:56.720 --> 00:59.320
And after that we can go and specify static values.

00:59.320 --> 01:04.810
But since we are talking about the subqueries the values can come from another query.

01:04.810 --> 01:08.210
So here we have another Select statement from table two.

01:08.250 --> 01:10.290
And we filter the data for this query.

01:10.290 --> 01:17.050
And now the result of this subquery can be used in order to filter the data using the in operator.

01:17.050 --> 01:22.850
And now the big difference between the in operator and the comparison operator that's the subquery is

01:22.850 --> 01:25.090
allowed to have multiple rows.

01:25.090 --> 01:31.970
So there is no rule about having like one single value scalar subquery we can have in the result a list

01:31.970 --> 01:33.530
of multiple values.

01:33.530 --> 01:37.810
So this is the syntax of the subquery using the in operator.

01:37.850 --> 01:39.970
All right let's practice using this task.

01:40.130 --> 01:44.610
It says show the details of orders made by customers in Germany.

01:44.610 --> 01:46.530
So let's see how we can solve this task.

01:46.530 --> 01:49.170
First it needs the details of orders.

01:49.410 --> 01:54.170
So as we know we have the table sales orders.

01:54.290 --> 01:55.650
So let's go and execute it.

01:55.650 --> 01:59.330
So in the output we have all orders and with all details.

01:59.330 --> 02:01.730
But for the task we don't need all the orders.

02:01.730 --> 02:05.050
We need only the orders that made by customers from Germany.

02:05.050 --> 02:10.020
So now if you check the table orders you don't find any information about the countries rights.

02:10.020 --> 02:12.180
So we have to go and get it from another table.

02:12.180 --> 02:16.660
And as we know we can find these informations in the table customers.

02:16.660 --> 02:18.220
So let's build another query.

02:18.260 --> 02:25.820
So let's say select star from sales customers like this.

02:25.820 --> 02:29.420
So let's go and execute only the second query like this.

02:29.420 --> 02:32.700
Now as you can see in the customers we have the country column.

02:32.700 --> 02:34.380
And this is exactly what we need.

02:34.380 --> 02:37.460
So now let's make a list of all customers from Germany.

02:37.460 --> 02:39.060
So we don't need all customers.

02:39.060 --> 02:40.900
We need only the ones that come from Germany.

02:40.900 --> 02:43.580
That's why we're going to go and use the Where clause.

02:43.700 --> 02:48.300
And we say country equal to the value Germany like this.

02:48.660 --> 02:52.860
So let's go and execute it again and check the results.

02:52.900 --> 02:57.220
Now in the output we have our German customers number one and number four.

02:57.260 --> 03:01.180
So now we're going to go and use this information in order to filter the table orders.

03:01.180 --> 03:04.020
So let's go back to the table orders over here.

03:04.020 --> 03:06.580
And here we have the customer ID informations.

03:06.580 --> 03:10.940
And as you can see we need the orders where the customer is either 1 or 4.

03:10.980 --> 03:17.390
Now In order to filter that, we're going to go to the first query and use the Where clause like this

03:17.830 --> 03:19.910
and say the customer ID.

03:20.070 --> 03:25.350
So now since we have like two values one and four, we can go and use the operator in.

03:25.470 --> 03:28.430
So let's go and use the in and let's go and build the list.

03:28.430 --> 03:31.670
So let's go and have the one and four.

03:31.710 --> 03:34.070
So let's go and execute it.

03:34.110 --> 03:35.510
Now we can see the results.

03:35.510 --> 03:39.870
We have the orders but only from the customers one and four.

03:39.910 --> 03:41.350
So with that we have solved the task.

03:41.350 --> 03:44.510
We have the details of orders made by customers in Germany.

03:44.510 --> 03:44.950
Right.

03:44.950 --> 03:50.030
And now of course this is really bad solution because what about if we get like in the future a new

03:50.030 --> 03:54.550
customer, you don't want to go and keep adding here like values and so on.

03:54.550 --> 04:00.470
For each time you have a new customer, we want to make the values for this list to be dynamic.

04:00.470 --> 04:02.270
So we don't need a static value.

04:02.310 --> 04:04.190
We need like dynamic values.

04:04.350 --> 04:08.150
And we can use the subqueries in order to retrieve those informations.

04:08.150 --> 04:08.550
Right.

04:08.550 --> 04:10.830
And we have it already in the second query.

04:10.830 --> 04:13.310
So let's go back to the second query over here.

04:13.310 --> 04:15.770
We need only those two values one and four.

04:15.810 --> 04:21.610
That's why we're going to go to the query and say, okay, let's retrieve the customer ID.

04:21.610 --> 04:23.970
So let's go and execute it again.

04:23.970 --> 04:28.210
And with that we have the one and four exactly like we have it here in the first query.

04:28.250 --> 04:32.730
And of course in the future if there is like another customer that come from Germany, this is going

04:32.730 --> 04:33.890
to be a little bit longer.

04:33.890 --> 04:40.250
So this query can always retrieve all the customer IDs that have the country equal to Germany.

04:40.290 --> 04:44.010
So now what we're going to do, we're going to take this as a subquery.

04:44.850 --> 04:46.730
Let's go and get everything from it.

04:46.730 --> 04:49.450
And now put it instead of those static values.

04:49.450 --> 04:54.810
So of course we're going to go now and put few spaces to the right side in order to understand this

04:54.810 --> 04:55.850
is subquery.

04:56.090 --> 04:58.330
And of course here we don't use any aliases.

04:58.330 --> 04:59.690
So now what we are doing.

04:59.810 --> 05:06.010
The results from this subquery can be used in order to filter our main query.

05:06.010 --> 05:12.450
So let me just call it main query like this and make this smaller.

05:12.450 --> 05:14.490
So let's go and execute it.

05:14.490 --> 05:16.170
And now we are getting the same results.

05:16.170 --> 05:21.580
We are getting all the orders from only the customers one and four where they come from, Germany.

05:21.580 --> 05:26.980
And these informations come dynamically from the subquery and we don't have to worry about new customers

05:26.980 --> 05:27.460
from Germany.

05:27.460 --> 05:29.380
It's going to be added here automatically.

05:29.380 --> 05:32.900
And this query can always return all the orders from Germany.

05:32.900 --> 05:37.060
So this is the power of the subquery together with the in operator.

05:37.060 --> 05:39.900
If you are having like multiple values multiple rows.

05:39.900 --> 05:41.220
So we have solved the task.

05:41.260 --> 05:41.580
Alright.

05:41.620 --> 05:42.300
Now one more thing.

05:42.300 --> 05:44.580
Let's say that the task is exactly the opposite.

05:44.580 --> 05:50.780
It says show the details of orders made by customers who don't come from Germany.

05:50.780 --> 05:52.980
So now here there is like two ways in order to do it.

05:52.980 --> 05:58.980
Either you go to the subquery and you say, you know what, the country should not be equal to Germany.

05:58.980 --> 06:04.540
So if you go and execute it, you will get all the customers IDs that are not from Germany.

06:04.540 --> 06:09.660
And if you execute the whole thing, you will get all the orders where the customers are not from Germany.

06:09.660 --> 06:15.780
So either you do that or you stay with the equal to Germany, but you go and convert the whole logic

06:15.780 --> 06:17.540
by using the operator nut.

06:17.540 --> 06:23.270
So now we are saying the customer id should not be equal to one of those values.

06:23.270 --> 06:26.110
So it should not be equal to 1 or 4.

06:26.150 --> 06:29.230
And for that we are using the not in operator.

06:29.230 --> 06:30.630
So let's go and execute it.

06:30.630 --> 06:36.750
So now with that we are getting all the orders where the customers don't come from Germany by just using

06:36.790 --> 06:37.470
not in.

06:37.510 --> 06:38.270
Operator.

06:38.270 --> 06:41.510
So that's all about the not in and the in operators.

06:41.550 --> 06:41.870
All right.

06:41.870 --> 06:44.950
So now let's see step by step how SQL execute our query.

06:44.950 --> 06:47.990
So we are targeting two tables the customers and the orders.

06:47.990 --> 06:51.590
So the first step is that SQL going to go and identify the subquery.

06:51.590 --> 06:53.590
And it's going to go and execute it.

06:53.590 --> 06:57.190
So the subquery here is filtering the data based on the country.

06:57.190 --> 06:58.990
So the query can be executed.

06:58.990 --> 07:02.030
And in the output we will get only two rows.

07:02.030 --> 07:03.950
So it is one column with multiple rows.

07:03.990 --> 07:05.710
This is the row subquery.

07:05.710 --> 07:09.990
And this is our intermediate results where it's going to be passed to the main query.

07:09.990 --> 07:11.590
So our main query going to look like this.

07:11.630 --> 07:14.190
We are selecting few informations from the orders.

07:14.190 --> 07:20.190
And we are filtering the table orders based on the customer ID where we are saying the customer ID must

07:20.190 --> 07:22.430
be one of those values 1 or 4.

07:22.470 --> 07:28.120
So the subquery here is supporting the main query with the information for the filter.

07:28.160 --> 07:32.520
Now, once SQL have everything, SQL can go and execute our main query.

07:32.520 --> 07:34.000
And this is going to be like the following.

07:34.120 --> 07:36.040
So we will start with the first row.

07:36.040 --> 07:38.440
And here the customer ID is equal to two.

07:38.480 --> 07:41.480
So the value two is not equal to 1 or 4.

07:41.720 --> 07:45.520
That's why this row will be excluded from the final results.

07:45.560 --> 07:47.280
Now let's move to the second row.

07:47.320 --> 07:48.920
We have here the value three.

07:49.280 --> 07:52.360
And the value three is not equal to one of those values.

07:52.480 --> 07:55.040
That's why this value is going to be as well failing.

07:55.120 --> 07:57.320
So we will not have it at the output.

07:57.360 --> 07:59.360
And then SQL going to go to the next one.

07:59.400 --> 08:03.840
Now this time the customer ID is one and it is equal to one of those values.

08:03.840 --> 08:04.960
It's equal to one.

08:04.960 --> 08:06.280
So we have a match.

08:06.280 --> 08:09.440
That's why this row will be included to the results.

08:09.440 --> 08:14.000
And the same thing for the next row because we have the customer ID one and so on.

08:14.040 --> 08:19.600
Now after SQL checking all those customer IDs, whether they are in the list 1 or 4, we will get the

08:19.600 --> 08:24.920
final results where we have here all the orders where the customer ID either 1 or 4.

08:24.960 --> 08:29.480
So this is how SQL executed the in operator using the subqueries.
